    pr_ref:  # 64bd
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-11 while correcting the Go arm of measurement 0002. Verified live on\ndev 1102e75bd3 by calling the production lookup directly with the REAL import\npath, which is the check the catalogue-reach probe structurally cannot perform\n(its fixture writes the catalogue's own module slot as the import, so it always\nagrees with itself).\n\nTHE DEFECT. go.yaml's third-party METHOD rows declare their module in a\npackage-IDENTIFIER vocabulary (`gin.Context`) while go.py emits the module PATH\n(`github.com/gin-gonic/gin`). The two never match, so the rows are unreachable\nfrom any real Go program:\n\n  lookup_with_module('JSON',  'github.com/gin-gonic/gin', cc='method') -> None\n  lookup_with_module('JSON',  'gin',                      cc='method') -> gin.Context.JSON\n  lookup_with_module('Serve', 'google.golang.org/grpc',   cc='method') -> None\n  lookup_with_module('Serve', 'grpc',                     cc='method') -> grpc.Server.Serve\n\nPOPULATION: 20 method rows — gin.Context 7, echo.Context 6, gin.Engine 2,\necho.Echo 2, fiber.App 2, grpc.Server 1. All 20 return None from their real\nimport path. Hand-written idiomatic fixtures with real module paths produce ZERO\nio edges for them, with a same-package positive control firing in the same run.\n\nWHY THE FUNCTION ROWS SURVIVE AND THE METHOD ROWS DO NOT — this is the part worth\nkeeping, because it is luck rather than design. `_module_matches` compares\ntrailing components, and `unix` IS the trailing component of\n`golang.org/x/sys/unix`, so the 33 third-party FUNCTION rows resolve fine from\ntheir real paths. A method row's module carries a TYPE QUALIFIER, `gin.Context`,\nwhich is the trailing component of no import path, so the suffix arm cannot\nrescue it. The catalogue's third-party function surface works by accident; its\nthird-party method surface cannot work at all.\n\nA COINCIDENCE THAT MUST NOT BE READ AS A FINDING. The taint/CFG plan separately\nrecords that go.yaml carries \"53 of 210 entries violating the strict-stdlib rule\nand was missed by the Plan C cull\". The set of entries the reach probe fabricates\nan import for ALSO numbers 53. They are NOT the same 53: they overlap in 50 and\ndiffer by `filepath` (stdlib, but written `filepath` rather than `path/filepath`,\nso unimportable as declared) versus `golang.org/x/sys/execabs` (third-party, but\npath-shaped and therefore faithfully imported). Two routes agreeing on a count is\nnot two routes agreeing on a set.\n\nTHIS IS A POLICY QUESTION BEFORE IT IS AN ENGINEERING ONE, AND THE POLICY ALREADY\nEXISTS. The standing owner ruling is that io_primitives stays STDLIB-SCOPED\n(ADR-0016 sec 27) and third-party surfaces are USER-SUPPLIED via overlays. All 20\nof these rows are third-party. So the question is not \"how do we make gin.Context\nmatch\" but \"why is gin in the shipped catalogue at all\", and the answer the ruling\ngives is that it should not be. Culling them is the deleting direction on paper\nand NOT in practice: they produce zero chains on real Go today, so removing them\nremoves nothing a user currently sees. That is the argument for the cull and it is\nalso the reason the cull is safe.\n\nNOT DONE HERE, deliberately: the cull itself. Removing 20 shipped catalogue rows\nis a catalogue-policy change and belongs in its own PR with the ADR-0016 sec 27\ncitation, an explicit before/after chain count on a real Go corpus asserting\nNON-DESTRUCTION (the zero must be shown, not assumed), and a decision about\nwhether the same vocabulary defect exists in the other 13 catalogues — which was\nNOT checked. Nothing here says anything about python.yaml, javascript.yaml or the\nrest.\n\nALSO UNRESOLVED: even with the vocabulary fixed, echo and fiber would still be\nunreachable for an unrelated reason — their import paths carry Go semantic import\nversioning (/v4, /v2), which defeats alias derivation entirely. Fixing the\ncatalogue vocabulary alone would move gin and grpc and leave echo and fiber where\nthey are. See the sibling item on the /vN alias defect."  # 64bd
    fields:  # 64bd
      statement: "A catalogued entry must declare its module in the same vocabulary the analyzer emits, or it is unreachable regardless of receiver syntax."  # 64bd
      root_cause: "go.yaml writes third-party method modules as package-identifier + type (gin.Context) while go.py emits the module path (github.com/gin-gonic/gin). _module_matches compares trailing components, which rescues single-component function rows (unix vs golang.org/x/sys/unix) but cannot rescue a type-qualified method row."  # 64bd
